    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Musa ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> at its territorial peak, when its wealth came from taxing and controlling the trade in salt and in gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambuk">Bambuk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bure">Bure</a>.</li><li>His pilgrimage of 1324–1325, with an enormous entourage and a vast supply of gold, made him known across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and most of what is known of him comes from Arabic writers who recorded it.</li><li>Arabic authors praised his generosity and justice, while Mandé oral tradition gives him little space and some of its performers say he wasted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s wealth.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Musa encouraged the belief that his wealth was inexhaustible by spreading rumours that gold grew like a plant in his kingdom. On the way home from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> he nonetheless ran out of money and had to borrow from Egyptian merchants.</p></aside>
    </section>

