    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">early 1300s</span><span class="rf-text">Musa becomes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> under unclear circumstances; by his own account his predecessor never returned from an expedition into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1324</span><span class="rf-text">On his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> he reaches the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in July, meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> and stays three months, giving and spending freely</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1324–1325</span><span class="rf-text">He completes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>; on the return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> many of his pilgrims die of cold, starvation or bandit raids, and he goes into debt to merchants</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1330</span><span class="rf-text">The kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> invades and conquers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>; Musa regains the city, builds a rampart and stone fort and stations a standing army there</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1337</span><span class="rf-text">According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, he sends an envoy to congratulate Abu al-Hasan Ali on the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, which took place in May of that year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1337</span><span class="rf-text">Musa dies; the date is uncertain, and by other reckonings he died in 1332 or earlier</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta.jpg" alt="Ibn Battuta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Handmade_oil_painting_reproduction_of_Ibn_Battuta_in_Egypt,_a_painting_by_Hippolyte_Leon_Benett..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Léon Benett, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The date of Musa's birth is unknown. He appears to have been a young man in 1324, and his birth is placed around 1280. His personal name, Musa, is the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>, "ruler" or "king" in Mandé, was the title of the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. In oral tradition and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> Chronicles he is also Kanku Musa, "Musa, son of Kanku", after the Mandé custom of prefixing a mother's name, though it is unclear whether that genealogy is literal. The Tarikh al-fattash claims that he accidentally killed Kanku at some point before his pilgrimage.</p><p>His parentage is disputed. According to Djibril Tamsir Niane, his father was Faga Leye, son of Abu Bakr, a brother of Sunjata, the first mansa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> calls him Musa ibn Abu Bakr, which may mean a son or a descendant of Abu Bakr, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited Mali under Musa's brother Sulayman, named his grandfather as Sariq Jata, perhaps another name for Sunjata. The historian Francois-Xavier Fauvelle has proposed that Musa was the son of Abu Bakr I, a grandson of Sunjata through his daughter, and that later attempts to erase a possibly illegitimate succession through the female line confused the sources.</p><p>The empire he was born into drew its wealth from the control and taxing of trade: salt from the north and, above all, gold panned and mined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambuk">Bambuk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bure">Bure</a> to the south. At his accession Mali consisted largely of the territory of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, which had become its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Musa came to power in the early 1300s under unclear circumstances, as a young man, possibly in his early twenties. By his own account, given in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, his predecessor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>, presumably Muhammad ibn Qu, launched two expeditions to explore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, the first of 200 ships and the second of 2,000. The mansa led the second himself and appointed Musa as his deputy to rule until he returned. When he did not, Musa was crowned, and the succession passed from the descendants of Sunjata to those of his brother Abu Bakr. Some modern historians doubt this version and suggest that Musa may have deposed his predecessor and devised the story of the voyage to explain how he took power; several others have taken the possibility of such a voyage seriously.</p><p>Given the scale of his later pilgrimage, he probably spent much of his early reign preparing for it. The gold he carried probably represented years of accumulated tribute. The preparations are likely to have included raids to capture and enslave people from neighbouring lands, since his entourage included many thousands of slaves; the historian Michael Gomez estimates that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> may have captured over 6,000 slaves a year for this purpose. Perhaps for this reason, his early reign was spent in continuous military conflict with neighbouring non-Muslim societies. In Cairo in 1324 Musa said that he had conquered 24 cities and their surrounding districts. When he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> he appointed his son Muhammad to rule in his absence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque.jpg" alt="Djinguereber Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djingareiber_cour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KaTeznik, CC BY-SA 2.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Musa made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> between 1324 and 1325. Arabic chroniclers writing afterwards give widely varying figures for his caravan, which modern historians treat as rhetorical rather than statistical: upwards of 12,000 slaves, each said to have carried 1.8 kg of gold bars, and 80 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> each said to have carried between 23 and 136 kg of gold dust. He is said to have given gold to the poor and to the cities along his route. He reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in July 1324, camped for three days by the Pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a> and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> on 19 July. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> expected him to prostrate himself; Musa at first refused, and when he did bow said he did so for God alone. The two rulers nevertheless exchanged gifts, and Musa left with the official caravan on 18 October. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> he persuaded his men to back down when swords were drawn between Malian and Turkic pilgrims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Masjid al-Haram</a>.</p><p>He expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s borders, bringing in Gao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> says both cities submitted as he passed on his return; one account in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> credits his general Saghmanja with conquering Gao, and another dates that conquest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-sakura">Mansa Sakura</a>. He sought closer ties with the Mamluk and Marinid sultanates and brought scholars to Mali, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> jurists and the Andalusi poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-al-sahili">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</a>. Timbuktu became a centre of trade and Islamic scholarship; the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankore-madrasah">Sankore</a> was restaffed with jurists, astronomers and mathematicians, and his reign is associated with a portion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/griots"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/griots.jpg" alt="Griots" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/griots">Griots</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GriotF%C3%AAte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jeanniot (grav.), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Musa's critics are found chiefly in Mandé oral tradition. The jeliw, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>, say comparatively little of him, criticise him as unfaithful to tradition, and some regard him as having wasted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s wealth. Fauvelle suggests that hostility towards Musa's branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita dynasty</a> would explain his relative absence from, or scathing treatment by, the oral histories.</p><p>The pilgrimage also ended badly. Travelling back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> apart from the main caravan, many of the Malian pilgrims died of cold, starvation or bandit raids before reaching Suez. Musa ran out of money, resold much of what he had bought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and borrowed from merchants such as Siraj al-Din at a high rate of interest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">Al-Umari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> state that the moneylenders were never repaid or only partly repaid; other sources disagree.</p><p>Later judgements of his wealth and works have been disputed as well. Al-Umari said that Musa's gifts lowered the value of a mithqal of gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 25 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> to less than 22 for at least twelve years. This has been described as having "wrecked" Egypt's economy, but the historian Warren Schultz argues that it was well within normal fluctuations. Hadrien Collet and other historians hold that his wealth cannot be calculated accurately, and conversions into modern money are regarded as methodologically unsound. J. O. Hunwick has shown that the only project firmly attributable to al-Sahili is a royal audience chamber, and modern scholarship regards the image of him as founder of West African mosque architecture as a historiographical myth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the years after the pilgrimage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> drew merchants from Hausaland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and other African kingdoms, and news of it reached southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> added the city to their maps. In 1330 the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> invaded and conquered Timbuktu. Musa quickly regained it, built a rampart and a stone fort, and placed a standing army there to protect it from future invaders.</p><p>The date of his death is uncertain. Counting back by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>'s reign lengths from the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> Suleyman in 1360, Musa would have died in 1332. Yet Ibn Khaldun also reports that Musa sent an envoy to congratulate Abu al-Hasan Ali on his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, which took place in May 1337, and that by the time an envoy came in reply Musa had died and Suleyman was on the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">Al-Umari</a>, writing in approximately 1337, twelve years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>, claimed that Musa returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> intending to abdicate and go back to live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, but died before he could do so, which suggests a date even earlier than 1332. Nehemia Levtzion regarded 1337 as the most likely date, and other scholars have accepted it.</p><p>The succession is tangled with the same problem. It may have been Musa's son Maghan who congratulated Abu al-Hasan or received his envoy, and Ibn Khaldun may have confused Musa's brother Suleyman with Maghan. Alternatively, the four-year reign that Ibn Khaldun credits to Maghan may refer to his rule while Musa was away on the hajj, with only a brief reign in his own right before Suleyman.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas.jpg" alt="Catalan Atlas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Compass_rose_from_Catalan_Atlas_(1375).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cresques Abraham, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Musa's reign is commonly regarded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s golden age, though this may reflect its being the reign best recorded by Arabic sources rather than his necessarily being the wealthiest and most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>. The empire's territory was at its height under Musa and his brother Sulayman, covering the Sudan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. His palace has vanished, but the mosque and university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> still stand.</p><p>In Mandé oral tradition some aspects of Musa appear to have passed into Fajigi, "father of hope", who is remembered as travelling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to retrieve the ceremonial objects called boliw and who combines <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with traditional beliefs. The name Musa has become virtually synonymous with pilgrimage, so that other figures remembered as pilgrims are also called Musa. Arabic writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> praised his generosity, virtue and intelligence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> wrote that he "was an upright man and a great king, and tales of his justice are still told".</p><p>Abroad he became "a symbol of fabulous wealth". A tradition of showing a wealthy West African monarch on European maps runs from Angelino Dulcert's map of 1339 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a> of 1375 to the Mansa Musa Map made by the Sicilian Jacopo Russo in 1525. Online articles in the 21st century have called him the richest person of all time, and Encyclopedia Britannica states that he is "widely considered to be the wealthiest person in history"; historians answer that the Arabic sources were probably expressing that he had more gold than they thought possible rather than giving an exact number.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mansa Musa ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> at its greatest extent in the early 14th century. His pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1324–1325 and his gifts of gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> made him famous in the Muslim world and on European maps, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> grew as a centre of trade and learning. Much about his birth, accession and death remains uncertain.</em></p>

